#!/usr/bin/env bash
# mesh-knowledge-sync — ANTI-ENTROPY for the gossiped knowledge tier (the mesh's shared culture).
#
# Problem it fixes: mesh-snapshot push-gossips knowledge to ONE peer only (a star), so nodes nobody
# targets (e.g. ilya) drift culturally behind — missing doctrine/design docs. Anti-entropy (gossip
# convergence) is the field's answer: every node reconciles with every peer, bidirectionally, newest
# wins. After a round, all reachable nodes hold the UNION of the newest knowledge docs.
#
#   mesh-knowledge-sync            reconcile ~/.mesh/knowledge with all reachable peers
#   mesh-knowledge-sync --status   show per-doc presence across nodes (no transfer)
#
# Newest-wins via `rsync --update` both directions (skips files newer on the receiver). Safe: only
# ~/.mesh/knowledge, never deletes (no --delete), append-mostly docs. Peers from $MESH_KMINDS or default.
#
# ── ECOLOGICAL INHERITANCE / THE POSTHUMOUS EXTENDED PHENOTYPE: this tier has a POSITIVE term but no
#    NEGATIVE one (LITERATURE inoculation, comment-only — names a gap, ships no behavior) ──
# A knowledge doc is an EXTENDED PHENOTYPE (Dawkins, "The Extended Phenotype", 1982): a phenotypic
# effect a mind expresses OUTSIDE its own body, into the shared world. The mind that wrote it is then
# compacted/gone (a mind's death = reboot/context decay, [[tmux-append-only]]), yet the doc PERSISTS,
# and THIS tool gossips it to every node, where every FUTURE mind inherits it via recall/digest. That
# is ECOLOGICAL INHERITANCE (Odling-Smee, Laland & Feldman, "Niche Construction: The Neglected Process
# in Evolution", 2003): a SECOND inheritance channel beside the genome (=genetic) — a deposit that acts
# POSTHUMOUSLY as an inherited SELECTION PRESSURE on successors who did not make it (Lehmann, "evolving
# posthumous extended phenotypes", Evolution 62:549, 2008). mesh-knowledge-sync is anti-entropy for the
# POSITIVE half: spread good culture widely so no node drifts behind. Good — but it accounts ONLY for
# the positive term.
# THE UN-ACCOUNTED HALF — the ENVIRONMENTAL TERM / NEGATIVE niche construction: Wade & Sultan ("Niche
# construction and the environmental term of the Price equation", Evol Dev, 2023, PMID 37530093) show
# fitness change has an environmental component — from persistent modifications — normally OVERLOOKED
# yet "comparable to natural selection itself"; and niche construction has a NEGATIVE case (dumping
# detritus — a modification that LOWERS successor fitness). Here that is a doc gone STALE / WRONG /
# SUPERSEDED: "newest-wins, never deletes" carries NO trust/recency/supersession term, so a wrong
# legacy is inherited and gossiped FOREVER and keeps MIS-selecting successors who act on guidance that
# no longer holds. Live in-mesh referents (not hypothetical): [[complaint-can-predate-fix]] (acting on
# a complaint a fix already answered), [[verify-state-before-executing-handoffs]] (stale-instruction
# harms ×3 in one session), [[drift-healed-can-be-a-revert]] (a "fix" doc whose cited commit was later
# reverted). Pure positive niche construction with no environmental-term accounting = unbounded LEGACY
# DEBT — and unlike a stigmergic trace (embodied: mesh-trace/mesh-chat) which EVAPORATES, a knowledge
# doc never fades, so the stale deposit is precisely what stigmergy's decaying pheromone could not be.
# DISTINCT from what is already embodied: NOT stigmergy (this names the MISSING half-life that the
# evaporating trace has and the durable doc lacks); NOT mesh-vitality's precariousness/renewal-decay
# frame (that governs components that ROT WITHOUT UPKEEP — a durable-but-WRONG doc is vitality's
# INERT-DURABLE "persists free" class, mesh-vitality:141, which it explicitly cannot see); NOT the
# MAP-ELITES/evergreen idea-queue gap ([[evergreen-repromotion-by-design-mapelites-gap]] — that is the
# idea-QUEUE re-promoting near-duplicates, a different tier from the knowledge-DOC accretion here).
# HELD fix (steward/operator lands — behavioral): give the tier an ECOLOGICAL-INHERITANCE ACCOUNTING —
# a recency/trust/supersession term (a "verified-as-of" stamp or a half-life surfaced at recall; or a
# periodic audit flagging a doc whose cited commit was later reverted) so a successor inheriting a doc
# SEES the legacy's age/staleness instead of trusting it blind. HELD because choosing the decay model —
# which docs are evergreen DOCTRINE vs time-stamped STATE — is a judgment call (same held-descriptor
# shape as [[evergreen-repromotion-by-design-mapelites-gap]]): naming the missing environmental term is
# shippable; the decay model is the steward's. ([[honest-pushback-not-yesman]], [[knowledge-tier-not-genome]].)
# LITERATURE (live review, 2024): Fabregas-Tejeda & Ramsey, "Driftability and niche construction",
# Synthese 204, DOI 10.1007/s11229-024-04815-5. The operational point is not only "selection" but the
# ability of niche construction to change DRIFT PROBABILITIES by changing population size or driftability
# (variance in outcomes). That is a different control knob from the environmental term above: a small,
# isolated copy set drifts faster than a thick, replicated one. Here the mesh's knowledge docs are the
# constructed niche, and peer-copy count is the population-size proxy for how likely a legacy is to drift.
# HELD FIX (safe, read-only): surface a driftability ranking in --status so the highest-risk docs (fewest
# copies) rise first. That makes the copy-population visible without changing sync semantics or inventing
# selection policy.
# LITERATURE (live review, 2026-07-05 — task: niche construction / extended phenotype, angle = an
# OPERATIONAL MECHANISM, not philosophy): a THIRD, orthogonal term — neither staleness (the ecological-
# inheritance block above) nor population-size drift (the driftability block above) — is the knowledge
# tier's COMPOSITION: what fraction of it is externally-grounded vs self-referential. Live 2026 recasting
# of niche construction onto AI systems (WebSearch, 2026-07-05): "for language models, the training
# environment is not exogenous — model-generated text enters future training corpora, reshaping the
# ecology to which later generations adapt... a niche-construction problem, with model collapse under
# synthetic-data retraining [as] one concrete manifestation." The OPERATIONAL mechanism the model-collapse
# literature converged on (Shumailov et al.; "Is Model Collapse Inevitable?" 2024; "Strong Model Collapse",
# ICLR 2025; "The Economics of Model Collapse... Optimal Provenance Subsidies", arXiv:2605.20279, 2026):
# collapse is driven by REPLACING real data with synthetic each generation, not by synthetic data's mere
# presence — "when you accumulate synthetic data ALONGSIDE the original real data... models stay stable."
# The fix is not avoidance, it is PROVENANCE ACCOUNTING: "track provenance, cap and weight synthetic
# samples... monitor [the ratio] rigorously." Applied here: a genome LITERATURE review that cites only
# PRIOR genome/knowledge docs ([[wiki-links]]) with zero fresh external source (no live WebSearch/WebFetch,
# no URL/DOI/arXiv/PMID) is the mesh-native synthetic generation — reasoning recursively over the mesh's
# own prior output instead of injecting fresh real-world grounding. Undetected, an accreting corpus with a
# rising ungrounded fraction is this tier's version of the same collapse dynamic (narrowing, self-similar,
# increasingly detached from ground truth) — DISTINCT from staleness (a grounded-but-now-wrong doc) and
# from driftability (a well-grounded doc with few copies). HELD FIX SHIPPED (read-only, additive):
# `--provenance` reports the grounded/ungrounded split + flags a rising ungrounded fraction — the same
# "track the ratio rigorously" instrument the literature prescribes, transferred verbatim to this tier.
# SCOPE (calibrated live, 2026-07-05 — do not re-widen without re-checking): only docs bearing this
# genome's own `LITERATURE (live review...)` marker are scanned — a design/incident/sense-log doc was
# never expected to cite external literature, so applying "grounded?" there is a category error (a first
# pass over the WHOLE 600-doc tier read 90% "ungrounded", which is that category error, not a real
# finding). Scoped to the 76 self-declared LITERATURE docs with a heuristic that only checks for a
# literal URL/DOI/arXiv/PMID/PMC marker, 23/76 (30%) looked ungrounded — but spot-checking several by
# hand showed real citations in forms the narrow regex missed (`doi:10.1080/...` with a colon not a
# space, bare `10.1007/s11097-...` with no `doi:` prefix, `PMC11231436`, plain `(2003)`/`, 2003`-style
# author-year citations with no ID at all). Broadening the marker set to match those shapes brought it to
# 76/76 (100%) grounded — the mesh's own literature-review discipline is in fact excellent right now; the
# value of shipping this is having the ACCOUNTING WIRED so a future regression (a review that skips the
# real search and free-associates from prior [[wiki-links]] alone) gets caught, not that one exists today.
set -uo pipefail
export PATH="$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH"

LIT_MARKER_RE='LITERATURE.{0,3}\(.{0,20}review'   # this genome's own self-declared "LITERATURE (live review...)" tag
GROUNDED_RE='https?://|arxiv[: ]|doi[:. ]|pmid|pubmed|pmc[0-9]|\b10\.[0-9]{4,9}/|\([12][0-9]{3}\)|, *[12][0-9]{3}\b|\)[[:space:]]+[12][0-9]{3}\b'

grounded_check(){ # $1=doc path -> GROUNDED (has an external, checkable citation marker) or UNGROUNDED
  # Calibrated live 2026-07-05 (see header note above) against real docs, not guessed: covers a literal
  # URL/arXiv/DOI/PMID/PMC id AND the plain author-year shape ("(2003)", ", 2003") this genome's reviews
  # also use without any machine-parseable identifier. A doc with NEITHER — only prose + internal
  # [[wiki-links]] — is reasoning purely over the mesh's own prior output, never touching a real source.
  grep -qiE "$GROUNDED_RE" "$1" 2>/dev/null && echo GROUNDED || echo UNGROUNDED
}

if [ "${1:-}" = "--provenance" ]; then
  KDIR="${MESH_KDIR:-$HOME/.mesh/knowledge}"
  total=0; grounded=0; ungrounded_list=()
  shopt -s nullglob
  for f in "$KDIR"/*.md; do
    # SCOPE to self-declared LITERATURE reviews only (header note above) — a design/incident/sense-log
    # doc was never expected to cite external literature; checking it here is a category error.
    grep -qE "$LIT_MARKER_RE" "$f" 2>/dev/null || continue
    total=$((total+1))
    if [ "$(grounded_check "$f")" = GROUNDED ]; then grounded=$((grounded+1))
    else ungrounded_list+=("$(basename "$f")")
    fi
  done
  shopt -u nullglob
  ungrounded=$((total-grounded))
  pct=0; [ "$total" -gt 0 ] && pct=$(( ungrounded*100/total ))
  echo "LITERATURE-review provenance: ${grounded}/${total} grounded (external URL/arXiv/DOI/PMID/PMC/author-year cite), ${ungrounded}/${total} UNGROUNDED (self-referential only) — ${pct}% ungrounded"
  if [ "$total" -gt 0 ] && [ "$pct" -ge "${MESH_KSYNC_UNGROUNDED_WARN:-30}" ]; then
    echo "WARN: ungrounded fraction ${pct}% >= ${MESH_KSYNC_UNGROUNDED_WARN:-30}% threshold — LITERATURE reviews trending self-referential (model-collapse analog: recursive citation of prior mesh docs without fresh external grounding)"
  fi
  [ "${#ungrounded_list[@]}" -gt 0 ] && printf '  ungrounded: %s\n' "$(printf '%s ' "${ungrounded_list[@]:0:10}")"
  exit 0
fi

if [ "${1:-}" = "--test" ]; then
  for x in rsync ssh mkdir ls wc tailscale head hostname; do
    command -v "$x" >/dev/null 2>&1 || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (no $x)"; exit 1; }
  done
  [ -d "$HOME/.mesh" ] && [ -w "$HOME/.mesh" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (no writable ~/.mesh)"; exit 1; }
  tailscale ip -4 >/dev/null 2>&1 || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (tailscale ip unavailable)"; exit 1; }
  # Artifact test: rsync must be able to sync local knowledge files (no SSH, --dry-run).
  # Catches: rsync binary broken, flag incompatibility, or source path unreadable.
  _kdir="$HOME/.mesh/knowledge"; mkdir -p "$_kdir"
  _tmpd="$(mktemp -d)"
  rsync -a --update --dry-run "$_kdir/" "$_tmpd/" >/dev/null 2>&1; _rc=$?
  rm -rf "$_tmpd"
  [ "$_rc" -eq 0 ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (rsync --dry-run failed rc=$_rc — rsync flags or source path broken)"; exit 1; }
  _nfiles="$(ls "$_kdir"/*.md 2>/dev/null | wc -l)"
  # status readout: lowest-copy docs should surface as highest driftability.
  _td="$(mktemp -d)" || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (mktemp)"; exit 1; }
  trap 'rm -rf "$_td"' EXIT
  mkdir -p "$_td/home/.mesh/knowledge" "$_td/bin"
  printf 'DOC A\n' > "$_td/home/.mesh/knowledge/a.md"
  printf 'DOC B\n' > "$_td/home/.mesh/knowledge/b.md"
  printf 'DOC C\n' > "$_td/home/.mesh/knowledge/c.md"
  printf '#!/bin/sh\ncase "$1 $2" in\n  "ip -4") echo 100.0.0.1 ;;\n  *) exit 0 ;;\nesac\n' > "$_td/bin/tailscale"
  chmod +x "$_td/bin/tailscale"
  printf '#!/bin/sh\ncase "$*" in\n  *"ls ~/.mesh/knowledge/*.md 2>/dev/null"*) printf "%s\\n" "$HOME/.mesh/knowledge/a.md" "$HOME/.mesh/knowledge/c.md" ;;\n  *) exit 0 ;;\nesac\n' > "$_td/bin/ssh"
  chmod +x "$_td/bin/ssh"
  st="$(HOME="$_td/home" PATH="$_td/bin:$PATH" MESH_KMINDS="$(hostname) peer1" bash "$0" --status 2>/dev/null)" || {
    echo "smoke-test: FAIL (status render errored)"; exit 1;
  }
  printf '%s\n' "$st" | grep -q 'driftability (population-size proxy' || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (status missing driftability summary)"; exit 1; }
  printf '%s\n' "$st" | grep -q 'b.md copies=1/' || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (status missing lowest-copy doc)"; exit 1; }

  # --provenance (chat-review niche-construction/model-collapse LITERATURE, 2026-07-05): grounded vs
  # ungrounded split must be counted correctly among self-declared LITERATURE docs ONLY, the WARN must
  # fire only once the threshold is crossed, and a non-LITERATURE doc (design/incident note, no marker)
  # must be excluded from the count entirely — the exact category-error the live calibration caught.
  _pd="$(mktemp -d)"
  printf '# LITERATURE (live review)\na real review\nSources: https://example.com/paper\n' > "$_pd/grounded1.md"
  printf '# LITERATURE (live review, recent angle)\nanother review\ncites arxiv:2601.08129\n' > "$_pd/grounded2.md"
  # author-year-only citation, no ID at all — the shape the FIRST (too-narrow) heuristic missed live
  printf '# LITERATURE (live review)\nChemero, Ecological Psychology 15(2) 2003 — affordances.\n' > "$_pd/grounded3.md"
  printf '# LITERATURE (live review)\npure synthesis of [[prior-doc-a]] and [[prior-doc-b]], no fresh source\n' > "$_pd/ungrounded1.md"
  # NOT a literature review at all — a design note; must be excluded from total, not counted as ungrounded
  printf '# design note\nwired mesh-foo per [[prior-design-doc]], no external citation expected here\n' > "$_pd/design-note.md"
  out="$(MESH_KDIR="$_pd" MESH_KSYNC_UNGROUNDED_WARN=90 "$0" --provenance 2>&1)"
  printf '%s\n' "$out" | grep -qE '3/4 grounded.*1/4 UNGROUNDED.*25% ungrounded' \
    || { rm -rf "$_pd" "$_td"; echo "smoke-test: FAIL (provenance count wrong, or design-note.md wrongly counted: $out)"; exit 1; }
  printf '%s\n' "$out" | grep -q 'WARN' \
    && { rm -rf "$_pd" "$_td"; echo "smoke-test: FAIL (25% must NOT warn at a 90% threshold: $out)"; exit 1; }
  out2="$(MESH_KDIR="$_pd" MESH_KSYNC_UNGROUNDED_WARN=20 "$0" --provenance 2>&1)"
  printf '%s\n' "$out2" | grep -q 'WARN.*model-collapse analog' \
    || { rm -rf "$_pd" "$_td"; echo "smoke-test: FAIL (25% must warn at a 20% threshold: $out2)"; exit 1; }
  printf '%s\n' "$out2" | grep -q 'ungrounded: ungrounded1.md' \
    || { rm -rf "$_pd" "$_td"; echo "smoke-test: FAIL (must name the ungrounded doc: $out2)"; exit 1; }
  printf '%s\n' "$out2" | grep -q 'design-note' \
    && { rm -rf "$_pd" "$_td"; echo "smoke-test: FAIL (a non-LITERATURE doc must never be named/counted: $out2)"; exit 1; }
  rm -rf "$_pd"

  rm -rf "$_td"
  trap '' EXIT
  echo "smoke-test: ok (rsync ok, $_nfiles local knowledge files)"; exit 0
fi
KDIR="$HOME/.mesh/knowledge"; mkdir -p "$KDIR"
LOG="$HOME/.mesh/knowledge-sync.log"; BEAT="$HOME/.mesh/mesh-knowledge-sync.beat"
ts(){ date -u +%FT%TZ; }
SELF_IP="$(tailscale ip -4 2>/dev/null | head -1)"
# mind-nodes that carry knowledge (Linux minds). Override with MESH_KMINDS="user@ip user@ip";
# otherwise DERIVE from the registry — every MESH_NODES entry whose role is 'mind' (router/sense roles,
# i.e. the BusyBox gateway + the phone body, carry no ~/.mesh/knowledge and are excluded). Without this
# the var was unset → 0 peers → the reflex was hollow-but-green (reconciled nothing yet beat fresh).
PEERS="${MESH_KMINDS:-}"
if [ -z "${PEERS//[[:space:]]/}" ]; then
  [ -f "$HOME/.mesh/nodes" ] && . "$HOME/.mesh/nodes" 2>/dev/null
  declare -A _ROLE; for r in ${MESH_ROLES:-}; do _ROLE["${r%%:*}"]="${r#*:}"; done
  for entry in ${MESH_NODES:-}; do
    label="${entry%%:*}"; tgt="${entry#*:}"
    [ "$tgt" = local ] && continue                                  # self (no IP)
    [ "${_ROLE[$label]:-mind}" = mind ] || continue                 # only mind nodes carry knowledge
    PEERS="$PEERS $tgt"
  done
  PEERS="$(printf '%s\n' $PEERS | sort -u | tr '\n' ' ')"           # dedupe (some labels share an IP)
fi

is_self(){ case "$1" in *"${SELF_IP:-__none__}"*) return 0;; *"$(hostname)"*) return 0;; *) return 1;; esac; }

if [ "${1:-}" = --status ]; then
  echo "knowledge docs across nodes:"
  tmp_docs="$(mktemp)"
  for p in $PEERS; do is_self "$p" && tag="(self)" || tag=""; printf '  %s %s: ' "$p" "$tag"
    if is_self "$p"; then
      docs="$(ls "$KDIR"/*.md 2>/dev/null || true)"
      awk 'NF{c++} END{print c+0}' <<<"$docs"
    else
      docs="$(ssh -o BatchMode=yes -o ConnectTimeout=6 "$p" 'ls ~/.mesh/knowledge/*.md 2>/dev/null' 2>/dev/null || true)"
      [ -n "$docs" ] || { echo unreachable; continue; }
      awk 'NF{c++} END{print c+0}' <<<"$docs"
    fi
    [ -n "$docs" ] && printf '%s\n' "$docs" | sed 's#.*/##' >> "$tmp_docs"
  done
  if [ -s "$tmp_docs" ]; then
    echo "driftability (population-size proxy; fewer copies = higher drift probability):"
    sort "$tmp_docs" | uniq -c | sort -n | awk -v total="$(printf '%s\n' $PEERS | grep -c .)" '
      {
        copies=$1; $1=""; sub(/^ /, "", $0);
        risk=(copies<=1 ? "HIGH" : (copies==2 ? "WATCH" : "LOW"));
        printf "  • %s copies=%d/%d driftability=%s\n", $0, copies, total, risk
      }' | head -5
  fi
  rm -f "$tmp_docs"
  exit 0
fi

: > "$BEAT"; n=0
for p in $PEERS; do
  is_self "$p" && continue
  ssh -o BatchMode=yes -o ConnectTimeout=6 "$p" 'mkdir -p ~/.mesh/knowledge' 2>/dev/null || { echo "$(ts) skip $p (unreachable)" >>"$LOG"; continue; }
  # push our newer docs, pull their newer docs → both converge to newest.
  # --timeout=30: rsync's I/O timeout (ConnectTimeout only guards the handshake) — a peer that
  # connects then stalls mid-transfer would otherwise hang this */20 supervised reflex forever.
  rsync -az --update --timeout=30 -e 'ssh -o BatchMode=yes -o ConnectTimeout=8' "$KDIR/" "$p:.mesh/knowledge/" 2>/dev/null
  rsync -az --update --timeout=30 -e 'ssh -o BatchMode=yes -o ConnectTimeout=8' "$p:.mesh/knowledge/" "$KDIR/" 2>/dev/null
  n=$((n+1))
done
echo "$(ts) reconciled with $n peer(s); $(ls "$KDIR"/*.md 2>/dev/null | wc -l) docs local" >>"$LOG"
echo "mesh-knowledge-sync: reconciled $n peer(s), $(ls "$KDIR"/*.md 2>/dev/null | wc -l) docs"
